# Florin's Grandson (side quest, docs/story/side_quests.md; NPC plan batch 3): Old Florin prays in the Village of
# Barovia's church that his grandson Tudorel, who went up the castle road two winters ago, doesn't come back; Florin told
# the boy the castle pays well. Tudorel is alive, a footman in Lady Wachter's house in Vallaki, in the count's livery,
# warm and ashamed, sending one unsigned coin a month by the carter. The party tells Florin the truth, a merciful lie, or
# half of it. A moral choice with no fight and no right answer; nothing here needs a roll. Reached from Old Florin
# (village_of_barovia/indoor_folk:mihai) and Tudorel at Wachterhaus (npc tudorel). Lines drafted by Gemini through the
# ai-bridge, edited; every line here is unvoiced for now.

# --- Old Florin in the church (indoor_folk:mihai jumps here) ---

~ florin_ask
Old Florin [weary]: Tudorel? He went up the castle road two winters back to ask the steward for work.
Old Florin: The roof was leaking through onto the straw, and a boy of sixteen eats like a horse.
Old Florin: People think I come here every morning to pray that he walks back through that door.
Old Florin [sad]: I know what walks down from that hill, so I pray he stays wherever he fell.
Narrator: He clasps his hands together on the pew in front of him, but you can still see them shaking.
* [Insight DC 12] (He's holding something back.) -> florin_secret_ok | florin_secret_miss
* We'll look for him. -> florin_asked

~ florin_secret_ok
Old Florin [weary]: We had boiled turnip peelings for three days running, and my knees had gone too stiff for the ditching.
Old Florin: I told him the castle pays in silver and never lacks for firewood.
Old Florin [sad]: I have not said that aloud to a living soul, not even the priest.
Old Florin: Do not waste your breath forgiving me for it, because I will not forgive myself.
-> florin_asked

~ florin_secret_miss
Narrator: An old man's folded hands, white at the knuckle. If there is more, he keeps it where the prayers are.
-> florin_asked

~ florin_asked
if not quest.florins_grandson
quest florins_grandson asked
endif
-> END

# --- Tudorel in Lady Wachter's parlour ---
# Wherever her household ends up he is reachable (sq-stuck): in Wachterhaus while she keeps it, in the burgomaster's
# mansion with her when she rules the town (the same talk), and in the market square once her house has fallen
# (tudorel_square, below). The party can be told Tudorel's whereabouts by nobody: they look where footmen stand.

~ tudorel
if quest.florins_grandson == done
-> tudorel_after
endif
if flag.tudorel_met
-> tudorel_menu
endif
set tudorel_met
Narrator: A young footman in good grey-and-black wool pours pale tea into thin porcelain cups without spilling a drop.
Tudorel: Dry your boots by the fender if you like; her ladyship does not mind the steam.
Narrator: He catches you looking at his face, and his hand pauses on the silver pot for just a breath.
Tudorel [weary]: I know what you are thinking, and you may think it, only keep your voices down in the hall.
Tudorel: I have been warm for two winters here, and there is mutton in the kitchen every Sunday.
Narrator: Fixed neatly to the high collar of his coat is a small silver crest, the raven and crown of the castle.
-> tudorel_menu

# Her ladyship gone from her house (stocks, lockup or exile): he waits in the square in the only coat he owns.
~ tudorel_square
if quest.florins_grandson == done
-> tudorel_after
endif
if flag.tudorel_met
-> tudorel_menu
endif
set tudorel_met
Narrator: A young footman in grey-and-black wool stands on the wet cobbles, clutching a small linen bundle to his chest.
Narrator: The mist smells of wet ash, and a small silver raven and crown on his collar catches the grey light.
Tudorel [weary]: If you have come for Lady Wachter's household, her ladyship is gone from the house, and the rest of us were told to wait here until someone decides what to do with us.
Tudorel [sad]: I would take the livery off, only it is the warmest coat I own and the wind is coming down from the mountain.
Tudorel: You are looking at me as if you know my face. I am sure I have never had the pleasure.
-> tudorel_menu

# After the coin was found: the thanks differs when Florin was told the boy was dead.
~ tudorel_after
if flag.florin_told == "lie"
Tudorel [sad]: Thank you for going all that way, and for giving me a clean end in his ears.
Tudorel [weary]: Old Kolya will still carry the silver piece in its bit of tea-paper every month, unsigned, for as long as I have wages to send.
Tudorel: It is a shameful thing to say, I know, but I would rather he pray for a dead boy than curse a living one's coat.
-> END
endif
Tudorel: Thank you for going. The coin goes out on the first of the month, and now it goes out with a name on it, in a manner of speaking.
-> END

# Only while the quest is open at Asked: a party that never met Old Florin has nothing to say about him.
~ tudorel_menu
* [if quest.florins_grandson == asked] We've come from Old Florin, in the Village of Barovia. -> tudorel_coin
* Goodbye. -> END

~ tudorel_coin
quest florins_grandson found
Narrator: When you mention the old man in the village church, the boy goes quite still.
Tudorel [sad]: I give Old Kolya a silver piece every month when he goes down the valley with his cart, wrapped in a bit of tea-paper with no name on it.
Tudorel: If my grandfather knew whose coat I was wearing to earn it, he would throw it in the well.
Tudorel [afraid]: Please do not tell him where you saw me, or whose bread I am eating.
Tudorel: Tell him I died clean in the ditch if you have to, or whatever you think will let him sleep.
-> END

# --- Back to Old Florin (indoor_folk:mihai jumps here once Tudorel is found) ---

~ florin_choice
* Tell him the whole truth: Tudorel is alive, in the count's livery, and likes it. -> florin_truth
* Tell him Tudorel died, at peace. -> florin_lie
* Tell him Tudorel is well and warm, and don't say whose livery. -> florin_half
* Not yet. -> END

~ florin_truth
set florin_told = "truth"
quest florins_grandson done
Narrator: Old Florin sits very straight on the narrow bench for a long moment after you finish speaking.
Old Florin [weary]: Well. Better a grandson in a count's coat than a grandson in a count's cellar, I suppose.
Old Florin [angry]: The young fool always did care more for a warm belly than a clean name.
Old Florin [sad]: And the Morninglord help me, I am glad he has the mutton.
Old Florin: Wait a moment before you go back down the road.
Old Florin [smile]: Do any of you happen to have a stub of pencil I could borrow?
approve godrick_pendlebrook +1 thistle +1 liriel_dawnsong +1: You told Old Florin the truth about his grandson
-> END

~ florin_lie
set florin_told = "lie"
quest florins_grandson done
Narrator: Old Florin takes the news like a blow he has been bracing his shoulders against for two years.
Old Florin [sad]: Thank you for coming all this way to tell me plainly.
Old Florin: In my heart I knew it before the first snow melted, though a man still looks at the road.
Old Florin [weary]: It is a great courtesy to know he is at peace and out of the cold.
Old Florin: Would you mind lighting one of the tallow candles by the door on your way out?
approve kip_smudgewick +1 wren_featherfoot +1 godrick_pendlebrook -1: You told Old Florin his grandson was dead
-> END

~ florin_half
set florin_told = "half"
quest florins_grandson done
Narrator: Old Florin looks at your faces for a long, quiet time without blinking.
Old Florin [weary]: Warm, fed, and in honest service, and you did not think to ask the master's name.
Old Florin: That is either a very good lie or a very careful truth.
Old Florin [sad]: At my age, I do not think I need to pry into which one you brought me.
Old Florin: Thank you for the kindness, travellers, and mind the ruts on the road back.
approve ratatoille +1 liriel_dawnsong +1: You told Old Florin his grandson was well and asked no more
-> END

~ florin_after
Narrator: Old Florin sits near the brazier, turning a single silver coin over and over between his crooked fingers.
Old Florin [weary]: Old Kolya left a twist of paper on my windowsill this morning before the mist lifted.
Old Florin [smile]: Strange thing about castle silver: the paper it comes in always smells of good tea.
-> END

# Old Florin after the merciful lie: he believes the boy is dead, so no coin and no tea-paper (sq-stuck, finding 120).
~ florin_after_lie
Old Florin: I bought a fresh tallow candle for the stand by the door this morning, and the smoke goes up straight enough.
Old Florin [sad]: For two winters I asked the Morninglord to keep the boy from coming down the road, and now I ask only that he rest easy.
Old Florin [weary]: A man ought to feel heavier when he hears his grandson is in the earth, and I am ashamed to say my chest feels lighter.
Old Florin: Thank you for looking in on an old man, friends. The church is cold, even with the tallow burning.
-> END
